  "query_preview": "..\nMonday through Tuesday: A vertically stacked low off the\nWashington coast will weaken and slide southeast today (Monday).\nAs it does so increased lift ahead of the low combined with mid\nlevel instability will result in a band of rain showers passing\nthrough the region with snow levels around 5000 to 6000 feet. As\nthe low exits Monday Night residual moisture and upslope flow into\nthe Cascades, Northeast Washington, and the Idaho Panhandle will\nkeep showers persisting. With the weakening nature of the low and\nquick movement through the area precipitation amounts are \nexpected to be light, mainly between .05 to .15 inches. Exception\nis around the Blue Mountains into the Central Panhandle Mountains\nwhere stronger lift as well as surface based instability with \nCAPE of 200-400 J/KG has the potential to produce locally heavier \ndownpours with rain amounts of a quarter inch. A few thunderstorms\ncan not be ruled out but confidence remains too low to include in\nthe forecast.\n\nWednesday through Thursday: An upper ridge amplifies along 150W \nin the Eastern Pacific forcing a trough to settle into the region,\nwith a stronger closed low dropping south along the WA coast on \nWednesday and remaining in place Thursday. There is good ensemble \nagreement leading to high confidence of a cooler and showery \npattern as snow levels fall Wednesday to 3000-4500 feet. This \nwill allow for snow in the mountains including the passes, \nespecially in the Cascades. Increasing instability with 500mb \ntemperatures dropping to near -30C combined with upslope flow \ninto the Cascade crest will be favorable for several inches of \nsnow at Stevens Pass and Washington Pass. Below are the NBM \nchances for various snow thresholds being reached for the mountain\npasses from 5 PM Tuesday to 5 PM Thursday:\n\nMountain Pass   2+ inches   4+ inches   6+ inches   8+ inches\nStevens Pass    100%        75%         40%         5%\nWashington Pass 100%        60%         10%         2%\nSherman Pass     60%        10%          1%         0%\nLookout Pass     50%        10%          0%         0% \n\nSnow is likely to cause winter driving conditions at times in the\nmountains and drivers should be prepared for winter travel. With\nthe influence of the low and showers high temperatures will drop\ninto the mid 40s to low 50s, with overnight lows mainly in the\n30s.\n\nFriday through Sunday: The low remains off the coast Friday and\nSaturday but gradually weakens resulting in a drying trend.\nAlthough impulses rotating into the area will keep a chance for\nrain and mountain snow showers persisting. Snow levels overnight\nfall as low as 2000 feet in the northern valleys such that some\nwet snow could mix in at the lower elevations as well but little\nto no accumulations are expected at this time. On Sunday the low\ndigs further south leading to drier conditions across the Inland\nNW but with continued cool temperatures. JW",
